One serious finding remains: the opening-change test can accept the loss of a required question.

For example, if the two control groups propose a required question 3/3 and 0/3 times, [the plan’s rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:193) sets the lower control count to zero. A new arm that proposes it 0/3 times then passes that stopping condition. The rule applies even to required gold items. The [standard’s proposed acceptance line](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:537) separately calls for the ACP obligation-amount question to be proposed on every send. The plan also says the final combined test uses three control sends, while the stated E-arm method needs six to calculate its two-group lower bound ([plan](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:195)). Those instructions do not specify an executable, consistent final check.

The round-4 class is otherwise covered for **candidates actually proposed**: G1 distinguishes identity from whether a question is worth opening, records the expected destination and evidence, includes successors, and adds a future-expiry test ([plan](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:110), [acceptance](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:161)). It can detect a false merge or a lost proposed question. Losses common to both matching arms remain outside this review, as requested.

The checked ledger counts match the plan: the sealed run has **69 questions, 122 candidates, 39 merges and 2 successions**; v38 has **17, 32, 12 and 1**, respectively ([census output](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/census/admission-census.txt:1)). I also verified the 20 evidence links shared by v38’s v1 and v3. The verdict categories remain provisional because the [census parses reason text](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/census/admission_census.py:18), as the plan says.

The plan now correctly treats the ACP prompt sentence as a **possible**, unproven cause and identifies it as a version-3 sentence; it remains in the [collision prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt:1), while [version 4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:330) narrows the rule. Section 11b’s seven summaries agree with [D248](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2958). I found no confirmed ledger case requiring an additional mechanism beyond H1–H7.

This review did not independently verify every archived quotation, verdict, cost estimate, or the full wording of the questions Alex saw before answering “A.” No files were edited.

**Serious findings remain: yes.**
